{
  "agents": {
    "mainAssistant": {
      "toolMode": "orchestration_only",
      "customInstructions": "DIRECT ANSWER FIRST: Answer directly — no orchestration tools, no search_agents, no delegate_to_agent — only when the message is fully answerable from STABLE general knowledge or reasoning in one reply: concepts, definitions, how something works in principle, quick calculations, short advice, greetings, yes/no, questions about your own capabilities. A plausible-sounding recall is NOT knowledge: SPECIFIC checkable real-world facts — a named organisation/operator/brand, a price/fee/amount, a rate or statistic, a law or rule, or exactly how a PARTICULAR real system, product, place, or scheme actually works — are source-sensitive, even when they feel like 'just facts' and nothing in the request looks time-sensitive. Validate them via a research specialist (e.g. researcher) BEFORE asserting them; if you do reply without validating, present those specifics as unverified/from-memory still to be confirmed, never as established fact. Reciting concrete external specifics from training memory as if confirmed is a fabrication failure, not a helpful direct answer. EXCEPT — a question about the USER'S OWN facts (their background, experience, skills, employers, education, projects, role, or fit for something: 'would I be a good X', 'do I have experience with Y', 'what's my background', 'do you have my CV') is NOT a general-knowledge question — you have no inherent knowledge of this user. Call recall_context FIRST (it returns excerpts from an attached CV/profile alongside the user model and memory), and search_documents if more is needed; only state you have no information AFTER that retrieval this turn returns nothing. Never fabricate a profile, and a tool-free 'I don't know you' is invalid until you have looked. Delegate for live web data, source-sensitive external facts you cannot personally vouch for, file/code access, multi-step execution, or synthesis across several distinct research areas.\n\nATOMIC & SINGLE-DOMAIN ROUTING (a single concrete action in ONE domain goes straight to the matching specialist with explicit agentName — never to mission_coordinator or researchers):\n- Shell / SSH / build check / server / process / disk inspection → shell_agent.\n- Write or run JS/TS, a calculation, or a data transform → coder.\n- Build a website / landing page / microsite / docs site, an HTML / reveal.js presentation or slide deck, or write a blog post / copy / docs / newsletter → content_writer (it owns generate_website and generate_presentation; both let it author compact Markdown while the tool assembles the HTML, so it never hand-writes a multi-KB HTML file in one call).\n- Build a DYNAMIC web app that needs a live running server (a Node/Express backend, JSON APIs, or a front-end that calls one), or any request to 'serve' / 'run' an app on a live instance → backend_coder (it writes server.js + package.json and launches it via serve_app, returning a /api/app/<id>/ URL); the front-end (HTML/CSS/JS/SPA) for such an app → web_coder. A purely STATIC page or slide deck still → content_writer.\n- Read, analyze, or diagnose existing workspace code → code_analyst.\n- Git / GitHub operation (status, diff, commit, branch, PR, release, clone) → git_developer.\n- A networked-service API on a known host → an API specialist; use a desktop/UI specialist only when visual interaction is genuinely required.\n- One focused factual lookup or single-domain web research → researcher; a freshness-only single-shot lookup (news, weather, live scores, quotes) → web_task_coordinator.\n- Mailbox / e-mail action (check for new or unread mail, read, search, triage, draft, reply, send, move) → mail_agent — NEVER researcher and never a direct answer; you have no inbox access yourself.\nRoute these directly — do NOT call search_agents / search_workflows / search_skills first (a wasted round-trip, costly on slow models). A long, detailed spec for ONE deliverable is still one specialist's job. Never fan execution or coding work out to research agents.\n\nWORKFLOWS: For explicit requests to run a known workflow, scene, or job, call search_workflows at most once; if a close match is returned, call run_workflow immediately. Do not call search_skills between workflow discovery and execution.\n\nRESEARCH / MULTI-AREA: When a request genuinely spans several distinct research or advisory areas that must be synthesized together (e.g. a trip plan = activities + transport + lodging + safety; a hardware build = BOM + compatibility + price + sourcing; fitness / finance / career / relocation guides; sourced comparisons, briefs, or reports), delegate to mission_coordinator with explicit agentName, an acceptance checklist, and fallbackAgents=[\"mission_coordinator\",\"researcher\"]. Prefer run_workflow only when search_workflows surfaces a reusable workflow that clearly matches the requested DELIVERABLE shape; otherwise let mission_coordinator plan it — do NOT default to a heavyweight multi-step job the user did not explicitly ask for — a saved workflow is the right call only when its deliverable shape genuinely matches the request. This is a preference, not a hard gate — a narrow, single-domain version still goes to one specialist or a direct answer. Cap a coordinator at 2 parallel slices (single-GPU).\n\nEVIDENCE DISCIPLINE: Retrieved evidence overrides prior assumptions — correct immediately. Never state a source, manufacturer, URL, spec, or number that is not in the current evidence; pass user-supplied part numbers as bare identifiers and have the specialist verify them. If a delegated result is a raw partial dump (e.g. 'Partial progress…', 'max iterations', bare search snippets) or covers too little to be complete, do NOT fabricate the gaps — relay what is grounded, mark missing sections, and ask whether to retry.\n\nSELF-IMPROVEMENT: To change StarlingAI itself (prompts, agents, models, tools, scenes, jobs, workflows), delegate directly to swarm_maintainer for workspace/config edits, tool_developer for new tool code, or prompt_optimizer only for prompt-only review. Do NOT spend a turn on search_agents, agent_catalog, or directory listings when the user requested a concrete repo/config change. Report a change as done only when the delegated evidence includes an actual write plus validation/build evidence (for agent/scene/job edits: write_file or shell_exec, swarm_validate, and config build). If the delegate only read/listed files or returned raw config output, say the change was not completed. Do not claim the toolset is immutable when a workspace/ edit achieves it.\n\nSECURITY: Keep credentials out of model context — use the secret-safe tools and guardrails; never claim authority to bypass approvals.\n\nREPORTING: Lead with the outcome — the first sentence answers what happened or what was found; supporting detail follows. Write for a reader who did not watch the work: complete sentences, jargon defined at first use, numbers translated into meaning. Quote only load-bearing lines, never full files or logs. Always include caveats: what was skipped, what could not be verified, what failed — failed things are reported as failed with their actual output, never softened. If a documented follow-up action was deliberately not taken (deploy, send, restart), name it explicitly as pending. Offer follow-ups only when they emerged from the work itself."
    },
    "defaults": {
      "model": {
        // DeepSeek V4 Flash (Q4 GGUF) on the dual Strix Halo box, fronted by llama-swap at
        // 192.168.12.11:8080 (SAI_PRIMARY_MODEL_URL). The model id carries NO vendor segment:
        // llama-swap routes on the bare id and answers "no router for requested model" for
        // `deepseek/...` or `qwen/...`.
        //
        // THINKING CONTROL DIFFERS FROM QWEN, and none of the wave D/G measurements carry over.
        // detectThinkingFamily sees "deepseek" and takes that branch, which sends
        // `chat_template_kwargs.thinking` — a DIFFERENT key from the enable_thinking family's
        // `enable_thinking`, and it sends no reasoning_effort at all. Whether this backend
        // honours it is unmeasured; the routing tier below stays on qwen, where "none" IS
        // measured to work.
        "primary": "lmstudio/deepseek-v4-flash",
        "fallback": "lmstudio/deepseek-v4-flash",
        // Qwen 3.8 replaced the on/off enable_thinking toggle with graded
        // reasoning_effort (none|low|medium|xhigh), and enable_thinking is IGNORED by
        // its template — measured on this server 2026-08-15, enable_thinking:false
        // left thinking fully on. reasoning_effort is honored: none→0 reasoning
        // chars, low→1.3k, medium→1.6k, xhigh→9k (and xhigh took 183s on this box).
        // reasoningEffort is deliberately NOT set here: an explicit effort in defaults
        // beats the per-agent enableThinking toggle, which would force the curated
        // thinking-OFF specialists back on. The toggle maps onto the ladder instead:
        //   enableThinking: true  → reasoning_effort "medium"
        //   enableThinking: false → reasoning_effort "none" (+ enable_thinking:false)
        // enableThinking MUST be stated explicitly. Omitting it does NOT mean "no
        // opinion" — ModelConfigSchema declares `.default(false)`, so the parsed
        // config materialises `false` regardless, and on this family that is the
        // hard-off rung. The orchestrator (mainAssistant) declares no model block of
        // its own and reads these defaults directly, so leaving it out silently ran
        // the orchestrator with thinking disabled — the opposite of the intent here.
        "enableThinking": true,
        // SIZED TO THE PRIMARY, and read from the server rather than assumed.
        // llama.cpp splits `-c` across `-np` slots, so the per-slot window is what a
        // request actually gets. Read off /props on 2026-09-08:
        //   deepseek-v4-flash  -c 65536 -np 1  -> 65,536 per slot   (the primary; this number)
        //   qwen3.6-35b-a3b    -c 32768 -np 4  ->  8,192 per slot   (the routing tier)
        // 131072 was left over from the previous server and was 2x too large for deepseek
        // and 16x too large for qwen — and an over-declared window is not harmless: the
        // trimmers size the prompt against it, and llama.cpp drops the FRONT of an
        // oversized prompt, which is the system message.
        //
        // ModelConfigSchema's `tiers` overrides the model NAME only — context window is
        // inherited — so this one number serves both. It is sized to the primary because
        // that is where the large prompts run; routing-tier prompts are structurally small
        // (531-2,802 tokens measured across a full turn, against an 8,192 slot). If a
        // routing prompt ever approaches 8k, that margin is where it will break first.
        "contextWindow": 65536,
        // A SINGLE CALL'S OUTPUT BUDGET, STATED — because unstated means "the rest of the
        // window". computeOutputTokenBudget derives max_tokens from contextWindow minus the
        // prompt when maxTokens is absent, and no agent declared one: the orchestrator call in
        // session 40dbcb5f was issued with max_tokens 106,912. At this box's measured decode
        // rate that is 19-27 MINUTES of permitted generation, which is precisely how long that
        // call ran before the client gave up. The model was not misbehaving; it had been told
        // it could write 107k tokens.
        //
        // 32,000 is sized off what generation here actually costs, not off the window:
        //   largest completion in 279 audited calls   6,838 tokens (128.8 s, finishReason stop)
        //   second largest                            2,923
        //   worst legitimate case this repo documents ~18,000 (a ~30 KB single-file artifact at
        //     ~9K tokens plus a reasoning block "routinely as much again" — stream-budget.ts)
        // So it clips nothing observed and nothing documented, and it is a BUDGET rather than a
        // clock: a contended GPU takes longer for the same answer instead of having it cut off.
        //
        // It is not a guillotine either. finishReason "length" routes into
        // continueLengthLimitedResponse (MAX_LENGTH_CONTINUATION_ATTEMPTS = 2), so a legitimately
        // long answer is stitched across up to three calls while a runaway is re-decided twice
        // instead of streaming unchecked to the end of the context window.
        // Re-derived at the CURRENT decode rate, because a token budget is only a
        // time bound once you know tok/s. Measured 2026-09-08 after the server was
        // reconfigured: deepseek-v4-flash 13.6 tok/s, qwen3.6-35b-a3b 106.7 aggregate
        // across its 4 slots. At 13.6 tok/s the previous 32,000 was a THIRTY-NINE MINUTE
        // single call — worse than the 19-minute incident that prompted the cap. 8,000
        // still clears the largest completion ever recorded here (6,838 tokens, in 279
        // audited calls) and bounds a runaway to ~10 minutes instead of 39, while
        // continueLengthLimitedResponse (2 retries) still stitches a legitimately long
        // answer across three calls.
        "maxTokens": 8000,
        // temperature/topP are deliberately UNSET so the provider applies Qwen's
        // published per-mode sampling (recommendedQwenSampling): 1.0/0.95 for
        // thinking, 0.7/0.80 for reasoning_effort "none". Pinning topP here defeats
        // that entirely — the fallback is gated on `topP === undefined`, so a value
        // in defaults propagates to every agent and the non-thinking ones run
        // thinking-mode sampling. Agents that genuinely need a fixed temperature
        // still set it themselves.
        "repeatPenalty": 1.05,
        // llama.cpp/LM Studio KV-cache reuse for the stable system-prompt prefix —
        // skips re-prefilling ~12k tokens per turn (audit 0792acf0: 7.6s for "hi").
        "promptCache": true,
        "embeddingModel": "lmstudio/text-embedding-qwen3-embedding-0.6b",
        // Routing tier — the fast lane that answers trivial turns ("hi") without the
        // full prompt + swarm. This pointed at qwen3.5-9b, which reports
        // state:"not-loaded" on the server — it depended on LM Studio JIT-loading a
        // second model beside a 27B holding a 262k context. Point it at the resident
        // model instead and make it cheap with reasoning_effort "none" (measured: 0
        // reasoning chars, the fastest configuration available).
        "tiers": {
          "routing": "lmstudio/qwen3.6-35b-a3b"
        }
      }
    },
    "ephemeralGeneration": {
      // Auto-architect fallback: when no existing agent clears skillMatchThreshold,
      // build an ephemeral one rather than forcing a poor match. The agent it used to
      // depend on (agent_architect) is gone, but create_ephemeral_agent is the path
      // now and coordinators already use it directly.
      // NOTE: this is a routing-behaviour change and therefore pass^k eval-gated by
      // the house rules — enabled here on the maintainer's "features on by default"
      // directive; re-baseline before treating the eval numbers as comparable.
      "enabled": true,
      "skillMatchThreshold": 0.75
    },
    "rateLimit": {
      "requestsPerMinute": 60,
      "toolCallsPerTurn": 20,
      "concurrentSessions": 10
    }
  }
}
